+1. **Linear, constant-stratification Boussinesq branch.** Let $I\subseteq\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, use the flat periodic mathematical domain $\mathbb T^3=\mathbb R^3/(2\pi\mathbb Z)^3$ with fundamental cell $Q_3=[0,2\pi]^3$, and represent fields by $2\pi$-periodic lifts with the regularity specified next. Let $u:I\times\mathbb T^3\to\mathbb R^3$ be velocity, $b:I\times\mathbb T^3\to\mathbb R$ a buoyancy-acceleration perturbation in m s$^{-2}$, and $\pi:I\times\mathbb T^3\to\mathbb R$ pressure per reference density in m$^2$ s$^{-2}$; $u,b$ have continuous first time and spatial derivatives and continuous second spatial derivatives, and $\pi$ has continuous first spatial derivatives. The positive constant reference density $\rho_0$ is used in inertia and the incompressibility constraint, while density-anomaly effects are retained only through the vertical buoyancy acceleration $b$. The buoyancy equation of state is not specified here. The Boussinesq approximation assumes fractional density anomalies small enough to neglect their effect on inertia while retaining their gravitational buoyancy effect. In addition, this branch adopts a linearization about a resting, uniformly stratified state. Write $z=x_3$ for upward height and prescribe an affine background buoyancy-acceleration profile $B_0:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$, measured in $\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, with $B_0'(z)=N^2$ for the prescribed constant $N>0$ in $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The modeled total buoyancy anomaly relative to the constant-density gravitational reference is $B_0(z)+b(t,x)$; $b$ is its perturbation. Require the time-independent resting pressure $p_0$ to satisfy $\rho_0^{-1}\nabla_x p_0(x)=(B_0(x_3)-g)e_3$ on $U_{\rm bg}$. Thus $-\rho_0^{-1}\nabla_xp_0-ge_3+B_0e_3=0$ in the zero-velocity reference state, and subtracting it leaves $-\nabla\pi+be_3$ in the perturbation momentum equation. Equivalently, a constant-density hydrostatic reference has gradient $-\rho_0g e_3$, and the stratified resting